      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785708202038,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Muti-Step Processes",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Muti-Step Processes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XnRIHsNzAgF2ZCICD4U36",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 518.1171875,
      "y": 123.89058775748435,
      "width": 887.871337890625,
      "height": 860,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0r",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1055815987,
      "version": 1379,
      "versionNonce": 1522627581,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785711155626,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "One of the example complicated for handle multi process:\n\n1) Single server primitive\n-- microservice for each server\n-- Webhook, pubsub sync back to server when needed\n-- Manually building state machine, if server dies mid-order,\nthe row will sits there --> stall/lock state need to handle\n\n2) Saga pattern:\na/ Choreography: each worker subscribes for event it \ncares about, does it piece and emit event for next \nworker to react to. This work great for fully\nindependent services and mid-complexity workflow\nb/ Orchestration: single coordinator owns the flows\nand tell the worker what to do. Work for complex \nworkflow where we need for central control and visibility\n\n\n3) Event-driven choreography\n- Store events using durable log (Kafka/Redis stream)\n- Event sourcing treats this log as SOT, derive state from it,\nevent store entire history of systems and drive next steps.\n----------------------\n- Benefit:\n-- Fault tolerance:  Workers consume the log as a consumer group, \ncommitting their position only after an event is fully processed. \nIf a worker dies, its partitions are reassigned and the next worker resumes \nfrom the last committed position, so nothing is lost. \nSome events may get processed twice in the handoff, which is why workers need to be idempotent \n-- Scalability: add more workers to handle higher load, up to the topic's partition count.\n-- Observability: log itself is a complete audit trail of everything that happened.\n-- Flexibility: Appending a new reaction is easy, since it's just a new worker consuming existing events. \n------------------------\n\n4) Workflow orchestration (Temporal)\n-- Instead of losing all progress when a server crashes or restarts, \ndurable execution systems automatically resume workflows from their last successful step on a new, running host.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "One of the example complicated for handle multi process:\n\n1) Single server primitive\n-- microservice for each server\n-- Webhook, pubsub sync back to server when needed\n-- Manually building state machine, if server dies mid-order,\nthe row will sits there --> stall/lock state need to handle\n\n2) Saga pattern:\na/ Choreography: each worker subscribes for event it \ncares about, does it piece and emit event for next \nworker to react to. This work great for fully\nindependent services and mid-complexity workflow\nb/ Orchestration: single coordinator owns the flows\nand tell the worker what to do. Work for complex \nworkflow where we need for central control and visibility\n\n\n3) Event-driven choreography\n- Store events using durable log (Kafka/Redis stream)\n- Event sourcing treats this log as SOT, derive state from it,\nevent store entire history of systems and drive next steps.\n----------------------\n- Benefit:\n-- Fault tolerance:  Workers consume the log as a consumer group, \ncommitting their position only after an event is fully processed. \nIf a worker dies, its partitions are reassigned and the next worker resumes \nfrom the last committed position, so nothing is lost. \nSome events may get processed twice in the handoff, which is why workers need to be idempotent \n-- Scalability: add more workers to handle higher load, up to the topic's partition count.\n-- Observability: log itself is a complete audit trail of everything that happened.\n-- Flexibility: Appending a new reaction is easy, since it's just a new worker consuming existing events. \n------------------------\n\n4) Workflow orchestration (Temporal)\n-- Instead of losing all progress when a server crashes or restarts, \ndurable execution systems automatically resume workflows from their last successful step on a new, running host.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
